+## Pitfalls
 
-### Profile directory ownership
+1. **"command not found" via `docker exec`** — `/command/` (where s6-overlay puts its binaries) is on PATH only for processes spawned by the supervision tree — services, cont-init.d, main-wrapper.sh. `docker exec <c> s6-svstat …` will fail with "command not found"; always use the absolute path `/command/s6-svstat`. The `hermes` binary works because the Dockerfile adds `/opt/hermes/.venv/bin` to the runtime `ENV PATH`.
 
-The cont-init reconciler runs as hermes (`s6-setuidgid hermes` in `02-reconcile-profiles`). If a profile dir ends up root-owned (e.g. because `docker exec <c> hermes profile create …` ran as root by default), the reconciler can't read SOUL.md and fails with `PermissionError`. Mitigation: `stage2-hook.sh` chowns `$HERMES_HOME/profiles` to hermes on **every** boot, idempotently. Don't remove that block.
+2. **Profile directory ownership** — The cont-init reconciler runs as hermes (`s6-setuidgid hermes` in `02-reconcile-profiles`). If a profile dir ends up root-owned (e.g. because `docker exec <c> hermes profile create …` ran as root by default), the reconciler can't read SOUL.md and fails with `PermissionError`. Mitigation: `stage2-hook.sh` chowns `$HERMES_HOME/profiles` to hermes on **every** boot, idempotently. Don't remove that block.
 
-### Files written by `docker exec` are root-owned
+3. **Files written by `docker exec` are root-owned** — `docker exec` defaults to root. Either pass `--user hermes` or rely on the stage2 chown sweep next reboot. Don't write files under `$HERMES_HOME/profiles/<name>/` as root manually — the next reconcile pass will sweep them but in-flight operations may hit perm errors.
 
-`docker exec` defaults to root. Either pass `--user hermes` or rely on the stage2 chown sweep next reboot. Don't write files under `$HERMES_HOME/profiles/<name>/` as root manually — the next reconcile pass will sweep them but in-flight operations may hit perm errors.
+4. **Service slot exists but s6-svstat says "s6-supervise not running"** — The service directory is on tmpfs and was wiped on container restart. Either the cont-init reconciler hasn't run yet (give it a moment after `docker restart`) or it failed. Check `docker logs <c> | grep '02-reconcile'`.
 
-### Service slot exists but s6-svstat says "s6-supervise not running"
+5. **Gateway starts then immediately exits (`down (exitcode 1)` in svstat)** — Most likely the profile has no model or auth configured. The service slot is correct — the gateway itself is unconfigured. Run `hermes -p <profile> setup` first. The s6 supervisor will keep restarting it; that's the desired behavior (when you fix the config, the next attempt succeeds and stays up).
 
-The service directory is on tmpfs and was wiped on container restart. Either the cont-init reconciler hasn't run yet (give it a moment after `docker restart`) or it failed. Check `docker logs <c> | grep '02-reconcile'`.
+6. **Reconciler skipped a profile** — The reconciler keys on the **presence of `SOUL.md`** as the "real profile" marker. `hermes profile create` always seeds it. If a profile dir is missing SOUL.md (stray directory, partial restore, backup-in-progress), the reconciler skips it intentionally. Add a `SOUL.md` (even empty) to opt back in.
 
-### Gateway starts then immediately exits (`down (exitcode 1)` in svstat)
+7. **"Help, the container exits 143!"** — Check whether something is invoking `s6-svscanctl -t` or `/run/s6/basedir/bin/halt` — both cause /init to begin stage 3 shutdown but return 143 (SIGTERM) rather than the desired exit code. This was the Phase 2 architecture pivot from A to B. For container shutdown with a real exit code, you must let the CMD (main-wrapper.sh) exit normally; do **not** try to control exit from a finish script.
 
-Most likely the profile has no model or auth configured. The service slot is correct — the gateway itself is unconfigured. Run `hermes -p <profile> setup` first. The s6 supervisor will keep restarting it; that's the desired behavior (when you fix the config, the next attempt succeeds and stays up).
+8. **Adding a new static service requires the dependency chain** — A new `docker/s6-rc.d/<name>/` directory needs four files: `type`, `run`, `dependencies.d/base`, and the bundle membership `user/contents.d/<name>`. Forgetting `dependencies.d/base` means the service starts before cont-init finishes; forgetting the contents.d file means s6 never loads it.
 
-### Reconciler skipped a profile
+9. **`s6-svc -d` doesn't persist across restarts** — The `down` flag set by `s6-svc -d` lives in the tmpfs `/run/service/` directory. After `docker restart`, all services re-evaluate from their persistent state. Use `hermes gateway stop` (which writes `gateway_state.json`) for persistent stop/start.
 
-The reconciler keys on the **presence of `SOUL.md`** as the "real profile" marker. `hermes profile create` always seeds it. If a profile dir is missing SOUL.md (stray directory, partial restore, backup-in-progress), the reconciler skips it intentionally. Add a `SOUL.md` (even empty) to opt back in.
+10. **The no-op `main-hermes` service is intentional** — Don't try to make it do real work. Its `sleep infinity` holds a slot so the s6-rc user bundle is valid. The actual hermes process runs as the container CMD (main-wrapper.sh), not as a supervised service. See "Why Architecture B" above for the rationale.
 
-### "Help, the container exits 143!"
+11. **`docker exec` PATH doesn't include s6 binaries** — Unlike services and cont-init scripts, `docker exec` sessions don't inherit the s6-overlay environment. Always prefix s6 commands with `/command/`: `docker exec <c> /command/s6-svstat ...`.
 
-Check whether something is invoking `s6-svscanctl -t` or `/run/s6/basedir/bin/halt` — both cause /init to begin stage 3 shutdown but return 143 (SIGTERM) rather than the desired exit code. This was the Phase 2 architecture pivot from A to B. For container shutdown with a real exit code, you must let the CMD (main-wrapper.sh) exit normally; do **not** try to control exit from a finish script.
+12. **tmpfs `/run/service/` is ephemeral** — Don't store configuration or state in the runtime service directories. The reconciler regenerates them from persistent state (`$HERMES_HOME/profiles/`) on every boot. Any manual changes to `/run/service/` are lost on restart.
 
